{"name":"Baton","hex":"#866f5a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baton","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A staff or truncheon, used for various purposes.","A ceremonial staff of a field marshal or a similar high-ranking military office."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To strike with a baton."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baton"}],"entries":[{"title":"Club (weapon)","blocks":[{"text":"A club is a short staff or stick, usually made of wood, wielded as a weapon or tool since prehistory. There are several examples of blunt-force trauma caused by clubs in the past, including at the site of Nataruk in Turkana, Kenya, described as the scene of a prehistoric conflict between bands of hunter-gatherers 10,000 years ago."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/%E6%AD%A6%E7%BB%8F%E6%80%BB%E8%A6%81%E4%BA%94_02.jpg/330px-%E6%AD%A6%E7%BB%8F%E6%80%BB%E8%A6%81%E4%BA%94_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Club_(weapon)","wikidata":"Q392326"},{"title":"Baton (law enforcement)","blocks":[{"text":"A baton is a roughly cylindrical club made of wood, rubber, plastic, or metal. It is carried as a compliance tool and defensive weapon by law-enforcement officers, correctional staff, security guards and military personnel. The name baton comes from the French bâton (stick), derived from Old French Baston, from Latin bastum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Chicago_Police_helmet_%26_billy-club_b.jpg/330px-Chicago_Police_helmet_%26_billy-club_b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baton_(law_enforcement)","wikidata":"Q121650"},{"title":"Baton (military)","blocks":[{"text":"The ceremonial baton is a short, thick stick-like object, typically in wood or metal, that is traditionally the sign of a field marshal or a similar high-ranking military officer, and carried as a piece of their uniform. The baton is distinguished from the swagger stick in being thicker and effectively without any practical function. A staff of office is rested on the ground; a baton is not. Unlike a royal sceptre that is crowned on one end with an eagle or globe, a baton is typically flat-ended."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Le_mar%C3%A9chal_Jourdan.jpg/330px-Le_mar%C3%A9chal_Jourdan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baton_(military)","wikidata":"Q2014830"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Chinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg/500px-Chinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Chinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg/1280px-Chinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Chinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg/500px-Chinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Truncheon, Yuan dynasty","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChinese_four_sided_truncheon_-_mace_%E9%93%81%E5%B0%BA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Bulletin_%281932%29_%2820430590681%29.jpg/500px-Bulletin_%281932%29_%2820430590681%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Bulletin_%281932%29_%2820430590681%29.jpg/1280px-Bulletin_%281932%29_%2820430590681%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu